// `algebra` — the library's first classes, and the instances the other
// `std` modules' carriers earn (docs/traits-design.md, issues/traits.md).
//
// Everything here is sugar over records and globals: a `class` is the record
// its fields describe, an `instance` is a namespace of ordinary declarations
// with the record assembled at the end. Nothing new reaches the kernel.
//
// COHERENCE, and why each registered instance is the one it is. There is at
// most one instance per (class, carrier), so a carrier with two structures
// has to nominate one and offer the rest as *named values*:
//
//   * `Monoid<Bool>` is CONJUNCTION. Disjunction is just as good an answer,
//     which is exactly why the choice has to be recorded rather than
//     inferred: `and` is the monoid a "does every element hold" fold wants,
//     and it is the one whose unit (`True`) is the value a vacuous fold
//     should return. Disjunction is `bool_or_monoid` below — a plain global
//     of type `Monoid<Bool>`, passed explicitly or `let`-bound to shadow the
//     registry locally.
//   * `Monoid<Nat>` is ADDITION, and it is deliberately the *same* `op` the
//     additive hierarchy registers, so `Nat::Monoid.op` and
//     `Nat::AddCommMonoid.plus` are one function and the two readings of a
//     `Nat` fold agree definitionally. The day `mul` lands in `std/nat` it
//     becomes the second structure, and by the Bool precedent above it
//     arrives as a named value, not as a second registration.
//   * `Monoid<List<A>>` is append at every element type — the one instance
//     here with no competitor, and the one that is generic.
//
// The additive hierarchy (`AddSemigroup` → `AddMonoid` → `AddCommMonoid`)
// is separate from `Monoid` on purpose: it is the numeric spelling, its
// fields are `zero`/`plus`, and it exists to be *extended* downward the day
// there are integers. `AddGroup`/`AddCommGroup` are not declared, because no
// carrier in this library has negation — declaring a class no instance can
// satisfy would be advertising, not library.

import bool;
import nat;
import list;
import option;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The classes.

// A monoid: an associative operation with a two-sided unit — and the
// semigroup it extends, which is where `op` is *declared*. The split is
// writable because dispatch is keyed on the field's owner
// (docs/traits-owner-dispatch-design.md): `x.op(y)` builds the goal
// `Semigroup<typeof(x)>` and the classes that merely inherit `op` are not
// candidates at all, so a hierarchy and receiver sugar are no longer
// alternatives. (They were, until 2026-08-08; this file declared `Monoid`
// flat and said so here.)
class Semigroup<A> {
    op: A -> A -> A,
    assoc: (x: A) -> (y: A) -> (z: A) -> op(op(x, y), z) = op(x, op(y, z)),
}

class Monoid<A> extends Semigroup<A> {
    unit: A,
    unit_l: (x: A) -> op(unit, x) = x,
    unit_r: (x: A) -> op(x, unit) = x,
}

// A value every type of the class has. No laws: `Default` is the class that
// exists to be *found*, which is the whole content of `or_default`.
class Default<A> {
    default: A,
}

// A decided order: a `Bool`-valued comparison, reflexive in the shape
// `std/nat` states its order in (`Le(n, m)` is `leb(n)(m) = True`, so a
// `Nat` proof of either shape is a proof of the other and `nat::le_refl`
// fills `le_refl` verbatim).
//
// The field is `le` and NOT `leb`, which is what `std/nat` calls the function
// filling it. A class field that shares a name with a global in scope can
// never be reached by a receiver: `x.leb(y)` is ambiguous between the member
// and the bare call, and the elaborator says so by name rather than picking
// (which is right — the two are different functions at the same spelling).
// Naming a field is therefore a *library* decision about the ambient
// namespace, not a local one. See issues/traits.md's log for the finding.
class Cmp<A> {
    le: A -> A -> Bool,
    le_refl: (x: A) -> le(x)(x) = True,
}

// The additive hierarchy. `plus` rather than `add` for the operation, for the
// same reason `Cmp` says `le`: `add` is `std/nat`'s ambient function name.
class AddSemigroup<G> {
    plus: G -> G -> G,
    plus_assoc: (x: G) -> (y: G) -> (z: G) -> plus(plus(x, y), z) = plus(x, plus(y, z)),
}

class AddMonoid<G> extends AddSemigroup<G> {
    zero: G,
    zero_plus: (x: G) -> plus(zero, x) = x,
    plus_zero: (x: G) -> plus(x, zero) = x,
}

class AddCommMonoid<G> extends AddMonoid<G> {
    plus_comm: (x: G) -> (y: G) -> plus(x, y) = plus(y, x),
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Instances at the library's carriers.
//
// Every one of these is written in LITERAL form — `field := global` — because
// every fact it needs was already proved in the module that owns the carrier.
// That is the form's purpose (docs/traits-design.md §3): assembling an
// instance out of the library's own names, rather than moving definitions
// into a block and leaving every existing caller of `append_assoc` to find it
// under a new one. Where a field is a one-liner nobody would name (`unit_r`
// for `add`, which is `refl`), it is written inline.

instance Monoid<Nat> {
    unit := 0,
    op := add,
    assoc := add_assoc,
    unit_l := add_left_zero,
    unit_r := λ (x: Nat) => refl,
}

instance Monoid<Bool> {
    unit := True,
    op := and,
    assoc := and_assoc,
    unit_l := and_true_l,
    unit_r := and_true_r,
}

// The second structure on `Bool`, as a named value rather than a second
// registration: the coherence policy in this file's header. Nothing resolves
// to it — a caller passes it, or `let`-binds it to shadow the registry.
fn bool_or_monoid() -> Monoid<Bool> {
    Monoid {
        unit: False,
        op: or,
        assoc: or_assoc,
        unit_l: or_false_l,
        unit_r: or_false_r,
    }
}

// The generic instance: one declaration, an instance at every element type.
instance<A> Monoid<List<A>> {
    unit := Nil,
    op := append,
    assoc := append_assoc,
    unit_l := λ (xs: List<A>) => refl,
    unit_r := append_nil,
}

instance Default<Nat> { default := 0 }
instance Default<Bool> { default := False }
instance<A> Default<List<A>> { default := Nil }
instance<A> Default<Option<A>> { default := None }

instance Cmp<Nat> { le := leb, le_refl := le_refl }
instance Cmp<Bool> { le := leb_bool, le_refl := leb_bool_refl }

// The additive hierarchy, once in each direction.
//
// `Nat` PARENT-FIRST: three declarations down the chain, each writing only
// what is new. `AddMonoid<Nat>` does not restate `plus`; it fills from the
// canonical `AddSemigroup<Nat>` by reference, so there is one `plus` on `Nat`
// and every path to it is that one (`Nat::AddCommMonoid.plus` is
// `Nat::AddSemigroup.plus`, by `refl` — pinned in the tests).

instance AddSemigroup<Nat> {
    plus := add,
    plus_assoc := add_assoc,
}

instance AddMonoid<Nat> {
    zero := 0,
    zero_plus := add_left_zero,
    plus_zero := λ (x: Nat) => refl,
}

instance AddCommMonoid<Nat> {
    plus_comm := add_comm,
}

// `Bool` CHILD-FIRST, with disjunction as the addition: one declaration, and
// the two ancestors are synthesized as its own projections and registered.
// Which is the direction that makes a deep hierarchy writable at all — the
// alternative is five declarations to say one thing.
instance AddCommMonoid<Bool> {
    plus := or,
    plus_assoc := or_assoc,
    zero := False,
    zero_plus := or_false_l,
    plus_zero := or_false_r,
    plus_comm := or_comm,
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The consumers: written once against a class, used at every carrier.

// The fold every monoid is for. NOTHING here names a dictionary: the
// recursive call finds the constraint binder in the room, `h.op(…)` dispatches
// from the receiver, and `A.unit` projects from the type
// (docs/traits-type-projection-design.md) — the same goal, the same resolver,
// the same binder answering all three. `h.op`'s goal is `Semigroup<A>` and the
// binder is a `Monoid<A>`, so what answers is the resolver's upcast
// (docs/traits-owner-dispatch-design.md §3) — at an open carrier, where no
// synthesized parent instance exists to be found instead.
fn mconcat<A: Monoid>(xs: List<A>) -> A {
    match xs {
        Nil => A.unit,
        Cons(h, t) => h.op(mconcat(t)),
    }
}

// Proved once against the class. Its two uses below are at unrelated carriers
// and neither re-proves anything. `A.unit_r` is a **proof** field projected
// from the type, which needs nothing of its own: a type projection is an
// ordinary field projection, so an operation and a law are the same move.
theorem mconcat_single<A: Monoid>(x: A): mconcat(Cons(x, Nil)) = x {
    A.unit_r(x)
}

// The order consumer: the class's `le` decides which of two values to keep.
// The branch is a separate `fn` for the reason `list_algos`'s `ins_pick` is
// one — a `match` on a stuck `Bool` is a scrutinee no equation can reach, and
// hoisting it makes one `j` enough. A class projection is exactly such a
// scrutinee, so the pattern is the same one, not a new one.
fn cmp_pick<A>(b: Bool, x: A, y: A) -> A { match b { False => x, True => y } }

// Written in the INLINE constraint form (issues/requires-placement-syntax.md
// §R2-A): none of these names the dictionary — `x.le(y)` dispatches from the
// receiver and `A.le_refl(x)` projects from the type — so there is nothing for
// a binder name to do, and `<A: Cmp>` says the whole of what is true about `A`.
//
// `A.le_refl` is also where the *other-reading* rule earns its keep:
// `std/nat`'s `le_refl` is an ambient global, and `x.le_refl()` on a receiver
// would be ambiguous against it. On a **type** subject it is not, because the
// desugaring would put a type in `le_refl`'s `Nat` slot — a reading the
// declaration refuses (docs/traits-type-projection-design.md §4).
fn max_of<A: Cmp>(x: A, y: A) -> A { cmp_pick(x.le(y), x, y) }

theorem max_of_true<A: Cmp>(x: A, y: A, p: x.le(y) = True):
    max_of(x, y) = y
{
    j(sym(p), λ z q => cmp_pick(z, x, y) = y, refl)
}

theorem max_of_self<A: Cmp>(x: A): max_of(x, x) = x {
    max_of_true(x, x, A.le_refl(x))
}

// `Default` earns its keep here: the caller of `or_default` names no
// dictionary and no fallback value. Spelled `:= infer` rather than
// `requires`, which is the same mechanism (docs/traits-layer2-design.md) with
// the binder where a defaulted argument goes.
fn or_default<A>(o: Option<A>, d: Default<A> := infer) -> A {
    unwrap_or(o, d.default)
}

// The hierarchy's generic consequence: a right unit follows from commutativity
// and the left one, so `AddMonoid`'s `plus_zero` is redundant *for a
// commutative monoid* — proved once here, at both carriers below.
//
// The ONE declaration in this file that keeps `requires`, and it keeps it for
// a measured reason rather than an expressive one. Written
// `<G: AddCommMonoid>` with `G.plus(x, G.zero)`, it elaborates — and this
// file's check goes from 0.7s to 25s (measured 2026-08-08, release, both
// spellings). `plus` is declared by `AddSemigroup` and `zero` by `AddMonoid`,
// so each type projection builds its goal at an ANCESTOR and is answered by
// the resolver's upcast, which at an open carrier assembles a fresh parent
// record over the child's projections (docs/traits-owner-dispatch-design.md
// §3, §7.1) — five assemblies in one small proof, each a Σ-tuple of law types,
// compared against one another by conversion. Naming the dictionary projects
// the CHILD's own flattened field and assembles nothing.
//
// `G::AddCommMonoid.plus(x, G::AddCommMonoid.zero)` is the anonymous spelling
// that is also fast (it names the exact dictionary, so no upcast), and it is
// what this becomes if the cost is not fixed at its source; the readable
// spelling is kept here as the standing motive for fixing it
// (docs/traits-type-projection-design.md §9.3).
theorem plus_zero_of_comm<G> requires a: AddCommMonoid<G> (x: G): a.plus(x, a.zero) = x {
    trans(a.plus_comm(x, a.zero), a.zero_plus(x))
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The specializations, one per way an instance can be found.

// The registry, at a concrete carrier: `Monoid<Nat>` is declared, so the
// dictionary is a reference to it and nothing is derived.
fn sum(ns: List<Nat>) -> Nat { mconcat(ns) }

fn all_of(bs: List<Bool>) -> Bool { mconcat(bs) }

// The SCOPE, beating the registry: `any_of` is the same fold at the same
// carrier under the *other* monoid, and it says so by binding it. A local of
// the goal type is step 1 of the search, so the registered conjunction is
// never consulted (docs/traits-layer2-design.md §4).
fn any_of(bs: List<Bool>) -> Bool {
    let m: Monoid<Bool> = bool_or_monoid();
    mconcat(bs)
}

// The GENERIC instance, at an **open** element type: the goal is
// `Monoid<List<A>>` with `A` this function's own parameter, so it is answered
// by backchaining through `instance<A> Monoid<List<A>>` and not by any
// declared value.
fn concat<A>(xss: List<List<A>>) -> List<A> { mconcat(xss) }

// The class lemma at two carriers. Neither of these re-proves anything: they
// are `mconcat_single` with the dictionary found — at `Nat` through the
// registry, at `Bool` likewise, and the two folds are different functions.
//
// The third carrier, `List<A>`, is NOT here: `mconcat_single` instantiated at
// a list carrier exhausts the kernel's conversion fuel, whichever way round
// the statement is written (issues/class-lemma-instantiation-fuel.md, with
// the twenty-line reproducer and the three probes that place the cost). The
// *uses* — `concat` above — are unaffected and cheap; it is the generic
// lemma's instantiation that dies. Nothing here works around it: the missing
// theorem is left missing so that the issue closing restores it.
theorem sum_single(n: Nat): sum(Cons(n, Nil)) = n { mconcat_single(n) }

theorem all_of_single(b: Bool): all_of(Cons(b, Nil)) = b { mconcat_single(b) }

// The hierarchy lemma at both of its carriers, likewise.
// Written with the receiver, which is what the flattening workaround cost
// until owner-keyed dispatch landed: `plus` is declared by `AddSemigroup` and
// inherited by the other two, so `x.plus(…)` has exactly one owner to build a
// goal at, at either carrier.
//
// The unit is `Nat.zero` rather than `Nat::AddMonoid.zero`: at a **concrete**
// carrier a type projection is the same sentence with the class inferred from
// the one that declares the field, answered by the registry rather than by a
// binder. The explicit spelling remains available and means the same term.
theorem nat_plus_zero(x: Nat): x.plus(Nat.zero) = x {
    plus_zero_of_comm(x)
}

theorem bool_plus_zero(x: Bool): x.plus(Bool.zero) = x {
    plus_zero_of_comm(x)
}
